[{:db/id "q-48596/selection/f2099ec612a8ba2ad1232fc0698fe5c14c2ca2169a9927b8ddd0c140cf8bf767" :answer/id #uuid "15619ebb-7757-52f1-954c-c8317bb330f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596/selection/caa1480127d61a1777b06d605f1adffc9022d9830425fbcfd75797676392e878" :answer/id #uuid "91145809-13a5-5277-9068-c7c7d1186a78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" :answer/id #uuid "6afad7fb-8207-5b5a-9568-8270188b9f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "e31e3fa9-5215-5d6b-b073-dbaa4c626589" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596/selection/017ec74e48021b90d41305ba8cdc32b5df2a5e0d6620fb190ddde80f6dd00a99" :answer/id #uuid "15aaf551-bf34-542d-9fc6-91318a842944" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e0662-f7c3-5743-b6d9-4a287a964e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48596/selection/017ec74e48021b90d41305ba8cdc32b5df2a5e0d6620fb190ddde80f6dd00a99" :answer-field/choices ["q-48596/selection/f2099ec612a8ba2ad1232fc0698fe5c14c2ca2169a9927b8ddd0c140cf8bf767" "q-48596/selection/caa1480127d61a1777b06d605f1adffc9022d9830425fbcfd75797676392e878" "q-48596/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" "q-48596/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" "q-48596/selection/017ec74e48021b90d41305ba8cdc32b5df2a5e0d6620fb190ddde80f6dd00a99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48596" :question/id #uuid "5f7d83bc-e215-56af-9039-1143690e2108" :question/math-academy-id "q-48596" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection/ea2041f20c4b14bff500247c5888e7e0eb5fe45d5d45df3195d04364542f52ba" :answer/id #uuid "9a05ffc8-d328-590c-8251-8b19351f45c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection/c404754c3a56e6275fb348852d227681c7496ded76f1b6144166f87b542b67e6" :answer/id #uuid "5c0624be-188f-505f-8d9a-3a6e3bd93f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" :answer/id #uuid "2cedfade-ee75-59e1-ba37-303041bc54f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection/7cf4e91d95f6a8427144bce069796351adc324feea5f9f9715075700674661e1" :answer/id #uuid "d268f45b-b3c1-52bd-b251-00bf5f5b3da2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection/39f508abc01e725e09148a85c8f1573b302895828a712352740012f54d43f896" :answer/id #uuid "d3d7b048-b736-5f00-8f74-3a2a1f611307" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7aac87-d32c-5242-bdef-f3accb76fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99050/selection/c404754c3a56e6275fb348852d227681c7496ded76f1b6144166f87b542b67e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-99050/selection/ea2041f20c4b14bff500247c5888e7e0eb5fe45d5d45df3195d04364542f52ba" "q-99050/selection/c404754c3a56e6275fb348852d227681c7496ded76f1b6144166f87b542b67e6" "q-99050/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" "q-99050/selection/7cf4e91d95f6a8427144bce069796351adc324feea5f9f9715075700674661e1" "q-99050/selection/39f508abc01e725e09148a85c8f1573b302895828a712352740012f54d43f896"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99050" :question/id #uuid "fb13e83e-9cd0-53cb-aaf0-21f542a77f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99050" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99050/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" :answer/id #uuid "ef2def8a-cf94-57a8-997e-089a9c22b797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection/b55c90d4d51d040d856ebfd6bcc121444b83ed84311a09e7531656602fd808c5" :answer/id #uuid "78a9a3dd-c88a-5730-8af8-49e3e561841a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection/d49d489b8643593ea6da23ce0954b46f7709c930833e7da7a68231c37da9ea83" :answer/id #uuid "b84874f0-d9f9-5284-b498-0d91ad0d6cea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection/60c54941db3d1ccb2d1cd736be55d4add09c1c126f6cafed9570d159d7853082" :answer/id #uuid "5b1d1a2e-5cdf-5c6a-ace8-818954c38398" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection/74014f0df4913244829447c9cdcb1269005c9cbabedb407a4f565e831942234d" :answer/id #uuid "4d92fd6d-c367-5859-8da2-c1a317efa08a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65add198-edef-57ec-9f82-e394376b4295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127390/selection/d49d489b8643593ea6da23ce0954b46f7709c930833e7da7a68231c37da9ea83" :answer-field/choices ["q-127390/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" "q-127390/selection/b55c90d4d51d040d856ebfd6bcc121444b83ed84311a09e7531656602fd808c5" "q-127390/selection/d49d489b8643593ea6da23ce0954b46f7709c930833e7da7a68231c37da9ea83" "q-127390/selection/60c54941db3d1ccb2d1cd736be55d4add09c1c126f6cafed9570d159d7853082" "q-127390/selection/74014f0df4913244829447c9cdcb1269005c9cbabedb407a4f565e831942234d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127390" :question/id #uuid "c236be5c-f7ce-577e-8440-1e61c10bf0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127390" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127390/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127390"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection/bd65c42062e552af552e86d7d8cf7a1babe664d5b3dd75c155b23373fc40d5c6" :answer/id #uuid "21d24e56-c1ed-50ff-85e3-f1206e4334ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection/a0db99d2d944cc4d33560b438772eb57479beba4c55821c1592b0f7d2cccf411" :answer/id #uuid "f3695305-1cab-5741-bf86-c34271fd5d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection/739cefcf73d0beba79016b9e7cd3be00bcfa5296bb2332df279466b80763e96d" :answer/id #uuid "8630c70d-ec2e-5494-9ac9-e24c2e41a159" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection/24d97b855d8b78b3ced9e9179d40e6febd9866ee6b299bbe36f9bac746f80d75" :answer/id #uuid "4039e1a7-cc37-51a3-ab37-2c71b8952690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection/a96b077ef1f3fcf5b0d0004814a169337184e4af3d39e7ae7e395f5b61ed1512" :answer/id #uuid "95225043-0ced-530d-8ce3-c6518410fa26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf076dfe-9ae6-539c-81af-8a6891189af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132464/selection/bd65c42062e552af552e86d7d8cf7a1babe664d5b3dd75c155b23373fc40d5c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-132464/selection/bd65c42062e552af552e86d7d8cf7a1babe664d5b3dd75c155b23373fc40d5c6" "q-132464/selection/a0db99d2d944cc4d33560b438772eb57479beba4c55821c1592b0f7d2cccf411" "q-132464/selection/739cefcf73d0beba79016b9e7cd3be00bcfa5296bb2332df279466b80763e96d" "q-132464/selection/24d97b855d8b78b3ced9e9179d40e6febd9866ee6b299bbe36f9bac746f80d75" "q-132464/selection/a96b077ef1f3fcf5b0d0004814a169337184e4af3d39e7ae7e395f5b61ed1512"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132464" :question/id #uuid "020c0b00-cb4a-56fb-b3c8-84c40dc80072" :question/math-academy-id "q-132464" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+6-2{x}^{2}+4x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{10} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-132464/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345505/field-1/15ee1929294c57282b671fa871d2a52363dd94a235cbfdbaee61c726eee5c56f" :answer/id #uuid "32d641ba-ee11-5d27-9896-21a463702ed9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345505/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d76069f-db56-5e1b-94b3-d7570368376d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345505/field-1/15ee1929294c57282b671fa871d2a52363dd94a235cbfdbaee61c726eee5c56f" :answer-field/choices ["q-345505/field-1/15ee1929294c57282b671fa871d2a52363dd94a235cbfdbaee61c726eee5c56f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345505/field-2/200261d947e50d663d5701d399a68419d8a92ca2712ab231b0710b35f4a3b672" :answer/id #uuid "4d691ab7-c99c-5721-b50e-52cbeb66846f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.74" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345505/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b41e47-202c-53ce-a2c4-9acccf3e190f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345505/field-2/200261d947e50d663d5701d399a68419d8a92ca2712ab231b0710b35f4a3b672" :answer-field/choices ["q-345505/field-2/200261d947e50d663d5701d399a68419d8a92ca2712ab231b0710b35f4a3b672"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345505" :question/id #uuid "400c8835-a3d5-5c89-91f7-0b22f3f1a5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-345505" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **approximate values**, rounded to **two decimal places**, of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-6)+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈-1.41$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x≈1.74$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-1.41,\\,x≈1.74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.41$ and $x≈1.74.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-1.41\\,\\text{and}\\,x=1.74\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345505/field-1" "q-345505/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345505"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
